title No anchor names the head
prompt The baton -- patchouli's lane, open the round, take the next agent-doable lap reds first
think Last lap closed %773 and named what its repair could not reach: the head reading catches a field's ARRIVAL and cannot read a sentence about BEHAVIOR. weave.rye's head carries exactly such a sentence about where a head insert lands, and nothing was pressing it. Pin the behavior, residue included.
obs THE MECHANISM, IN ONE PARAGRAPH. apply places an insert by four numbers -- run, site, order key, position -- and Place.less_than reads them in that order. An insert carrying an anchor in Diff.after inherits the anchor's run and order key and draws a fresh position, higher, so it sorts just past it. A null anchor keeps the counter's own answer: a fresh run, above every held run, so the line sorts last.
obs CLAIM 9 READS BOTH HALVES ON ONE PLANT. prove_head_insert_lands_last in mantra/src/weave_apply_witness.rye: a null anchor lands LAST, and anchoring on the FIRST line lands SECOND. Two asserts on the second half, because the anchor working is exactly what puts first place out of reach.
obs THE RUNNING FOUND WHAT THE READING HAD NOT. First place is unreachable from either spelling. A fresh weave's first line carries order key zero and the lowest position drawn, and the position is the LAST tiebreak, so a fresh position is always the highest. The residue is a property of the order law rather than a forgotten branch.
obs AND NO INITIALIZER FIXES IT, which is the tempting repair. Give a null anchor run zero and order key zero and the line shares all three leading fields with the first line; the position breaks the tie and puts it last again. The only mutation in the module that can put a fresh insert in first place is a change to Place.less_than itself.
obs SO THE CONTROL PLANTS EXACTLY THAT. Two new phases: anchor_ignored forces the anchor lookup false, run_order_reversed reverses the order law's first comparison. Legs 9 to 11. Both proven claim-9-specific in a pen -- with the leg removed from the witness, each plant walks free at exit 0, so this is the only reading in the file that presses either.
obs THREE DOORS PRICED, ONE RECOMMENDED WITH ITS FALSIFIER. Door A reserves the order-key-zero band and is a breach, since that key is occupied by the first line of every weave this tree has written. Door B adds a head flag read first, accreting as ord itself did. Door C gives every weave a document-start sentinel and makes a head insert an ordinary anchored insert. Door C recommended; the falsifier is a count of the places that must learn about a sentinel against the 12 place constructions Door B touches.
obs REDS FIRST, AND ONE RED WAS MINE TO CURE WITH ONE COMMAND. The cold endurance run named ryekey RED -- another spelling of one toolchain changed the key -- and rye/bin/rye stood 37,395 seconds behind its source, so my compiler predated %771's repair. Rebuilt through bin/rye.new and moved, since a running binary refuses to be written over. ryekey GREEN, 22 legs.
obs THE SECOND RED IS A PEER'S AND PROVEN SO. rune_assert_sweep reads 6,482 against 6,480, and it reads 6,482 at HEAD with my changes stashed away, with my file appearing nowhere in its detail. Pheromone's lane, live claim on it.
obs THEN THE STASH I TOOK TO PROVE THAT BECAME A RED OF ITS OWN. standing_equipment_run.sh reads tree_digest() at two points only, line 808 and line 1503, so a change made and undone inside a pass reads as a tree that never moved. The digest was sharpened from SHAPE to CONTENT at %221 and %380 and its SAMPLING was left exactly where it stood -- one axis moved, and the one that moved is the one that reads careful.
obs A SECOND HALF RIDES WITH IT. The transcript cannot say WHICH guards read which tree, because a guard line carries wall-seconds and CPU-milliseconds and no absolute stamp. So a mid-pass move stays unattributable even once it is known to have happened. Priced at one digest per guard boundary: 81 seconds over 310 guards at the 261ms that file's own header measured.
obs THE CARD'S OPEN-ROW LIST HAD DRIFTED AND IS RE-READ RATHER THAN REWRITTEN. It named seven OPEN; the pin's last bold marker per row reads 13 OPEN, 4 BOOKED, 3 CLOSED. Five of the seven stand on shelves, and reds_fold.sh refuses an OPEN row, so each was BOOKED or CLOSED before it folded. The elder sentence keeps its words as its own lap's reading.
obs THE PIN HAD 24 BYTES OF HEADROOM. 65,512 against 65,536, against a row needing thousands, so my own CLOSED %773 folded to its shelf first -- 62,051 -- and the new row brings it to 64,860.
